# StakMesh cluster launcher config.
#
# Copy this file to cluster_nodes.conf (gitignored - it'll have your real
# paths/usernames) and fill in your machines.
#
# Single source of truth for cluster membership: this file does NOT store
# hostnames. Rank -> host is looked up from the SAME topology file the
# mnist_distributed binary itself parses at runtime (configs/*.txt). This
# file only adds what that config can't know: which ranks run locally vs
# over ssh, each rank's remote build directory, and its binary filename
# (mnist_distributed vs mnist_distributed.exe).
#
# ── 1. Point at your topology file ──
# Same relative path you'd pass to --config by hand, e.g.:
topology ../configs/two_laptop_cluster.local.txt

# ── 2. One line per rank ──
#
#   <rank> local <build-dir> <binary>
#   <rank> ssh   <user> <build-dir> <binary>
#
# <build-dir> uses whatever path style that machine actually uses (forward
# slashes for Linux/macOS, backslashes for Windows) - this is also how the
# script knows which style to launch the binary with (./binary vs binary,
# posix `&&` chaining either way).
#
# Rank 0 (this machine) runs directly, no ssh needed:
0 local ~/StakMesh/build mnist_distributed

# Rank 1 (a Windows machine, reached over ssh). Two things to get right on
# the Windows side before this works:
#
#   1. OpenSSH Server must be enabled and running:
#        Settings -> Apps -> Optional Features -> Add a feature -> OpenSSH Server
#        (or:  Add-WindowsCapability -Online -Name OpenSSH.Server~~~~0.0.1.0
#         then  Start-Service sshd ; Set-Service -Name sshd -StartupType Automatic)
#      Test it works BEFORE trying this script:
#        ssh <user>@<windows-hostname>
#
#   2. Know which shell your ssh session drops you into by default - cmd.exe
#      accepts the "&&" chaining this script generates; PowerShell needs
#      ";" instead. Check with:
#        ssh <user>@<windows-hostname> "$PSVersionTable"
#      Prints a version table -> PowerShell (this script's generated "&&"
#      command will fail there on PowerShell 5.1; PowerShell 7+ does
#      support "&&"). Prints an error -> cmd.exe, which is what this
#      script assumes.
1 ssh <user> C:\path\to\StakMesh\build mnist_distributed.exe

# ── Optional: skip building on the remote entirely ──
#
# Add "<rank> deploy <local-binary-path>" for any ssh rank, then run
# `./launch_cluster.sh --deploy -- ...` instead of the plain form. Before
# any rank starts, this scp's <local-binary-path> (e.g. your own
# build/mnist_distributed, or a binary downloaded from a CI run - see
# .github/workflows/build-binaries.yml) to that rank's remote directory and
# binary name from its line above - the destination is never repeated by
# hand, so it can't drift out of sync with what the ssh line actually runs.
#
# 1 deploy ../build/mnist_distributed.exe